    "branch": "m3/array-filter-runs-20261102",
    "title": "Compact M3ArrayList survivor runs with a lazy rejection bitmap"
  },
  "review_statement": "## Problem and change\n\n`M3ArrayList.removeIf` eagerly allocated a rejection bitmap and copied surviving values one at a time. Allocate the bitmap on the first rejection, preserve the untouched prefix, and copy contiguous surviving runs with System.arraycopy.\n\nKeep stable element order, predicate exception atomicity, callback traces, mutation checks, and the declared API. Add seven regression methods and make the existing m3-jre21 test lane execute them.\n\n## Verification\n\n- Actual `m3-jre21` Maven module: **16 tests, zero failures/errors/skips**.\n- **2,147 exhaustive/boundary mask scenarios**.\n- Exact baseline and candidate tested under the default VM and `-Xint`.\n- Java 21 `-Xlint:all -Werror` and identical declared descriptors.\n\nThe M3JDK adaptation separately retains its newer <=128-element two-long mask path, value epochs, and bulk-add behavior. This is not a conversion of Guava linked-multimap and MapMaker entry-chain owners, and no speedup benchmark is claimed.\n\n## Canonical execution\n\nCanonical recipe: `com.synexia.m3jdk.CollectionsContractRefinement20261017`, source revision **7af83c5ee4ad166d8cd45c6c33822debabd705ca**, review https://github.com/hsoliwal/com.synexia/pull/15427. Product bytes were emitted by actual qualified SDK Results and the second receive changed zero files. The canonical recipe and executable receiver remain in Synexia.\n",
